est-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-327). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."," - 2026-10-18 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-278); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-278). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."," - 2026-10-18 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-300, LLR-322); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-345). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes.","+- 2026-10-18 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-278, LLR-310, LLR-338); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-278). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-943\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\acts.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":792,"oldLines":3,"newStart":792,"newLines":9,"lines":[" date = \"2026-10-18\""," approved = []"," reattested = [\"LLR-300\", \"LLR-322\", \"TC-345\"]","+","+[[act]]","+seq = 131","+date = \"2026-10-18\"","+approved = []","+reattested = [\"LLR-278\", \"LLR-310\", \"LLR-338\", \"TC-278\"]"]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-943\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\requirements\\low-level-requirements.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":2865,"oldLines":7,"newStart":2865,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"An adjudication re-attestation constrained at merge\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py\""," code_symbol = \"first_approval_scope/adjudication_approval_refusal/merge_approval_refusal/_registry_identity/lane_acted_set/_copy_lines/amendment_scope/reattested_between/_wrote_ledger/_added_acts/row_rulings/verdict_rulings/_tier_of/_below_approval/_held_row_line/_held_act_lines/held_reattest_refusal/reattest_scope_refusal\"","-detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal applies the additional adjudication bounds whenever any claimed row records a scope, including a mixed lane, and calls reattest_scope_refusal for that scope-bound lane and held_reattest_refusal for every lane. A malformed combined token is refused by name; an act-free delta has no scope refusal. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when that accepted verdict's parsed amendment row rulings do not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. Every approval act in the lane’s complete acted set - a live flip or born-approved row, a ledger-only approval, or a re-attestation - is refused when any row it approves lacks an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part or any row it re-attests lacks a MEANING or CLARITY ruling in an accepted amendment part among the branch's bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; a named verdict must also judge every row its act re-attests, and acts already on trunk are not re-read. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the complete acted set holds no approved or re-attested row in that registry identity, a row the act ledger names being placed in the registry its id's tier names whether or not the row remains in the live registry at the head, so a retirement authorizes its own registry's copy: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\"","+detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal applies the additional adjudication bounds whenever any claimed row records a scope, including a mixed lane, and calls reattest_scope_refusal for that scope-bound lane and held_reattest_refusal for every lane. A malformed combined token is refused by name; an act-free delta has no scope refusal. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when that accepted verdict's parsed amendment row rulings do not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. Every approval act in the lane’s complete acted set - a live flip or born-approved row, a ledger-only approval, or a re-attestation - is refused when any row it approves lacks an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part or any row it re-attests lacks a CLARITY ruling, or a MEANING ruling carrying the bless mark, in an accepted amendment part among the branch's bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; a named verdict must also authorize every row its act re-attests in the same way, and acts already on trunk are not re-read. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the complete acted set holds no approved or re-attested row in that registry identity, a row the act ledger names being placed in the registry its id's tier names whether or not the row remains in the live registry at the head, so a retirement authorizes its own registry's copy: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\""," test_refs = \"TC-278; TC-331\""," rationale = \"\"\"At merge the first-approval scope check read flips alone, and a re-attestation moves no cell, so an amendment act could re-anchor rows its verdict never ruled. The act ledger is the one record naming the rows each act re-attested, which makes the scope checkable where the merge is judged; a scope-bound lane claiming no amendment has ruled no amended text, so it may re-anchor none.\"\"\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3295,"oldLines":7,"newStart":3295,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"Combined sitting composes and validates kind-scoped adjudications\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/adjudicate_brief.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/sitting.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py;project-trajectory/scripts/session_service.py;project-trajectory/scripts/coordinator_adjudicate.py\""," code_symbol = \"requested_kinds/_sitting_scopes/combined_values;scopes/sections/own_section/declared_brief/scope_tokens/claim_scope/brief_scope/bounds_acts/malformed_refusal/GRAMMAR/DONE_WHEN_OUTCOMES/line_refusal/_keyword_lines/_machine_line/_incomplete/REVIEW/_folded_lines/review_line/_keyword_count_refusal/named_kinds/sitting_refusal/parse/requested_path/render_requested/read_requested/REQUESTED_SUFFIX/OUTCOMES/write_binding/accepted/accepted_part/accepted_at/judged_rows/_named_line/unaccepted_refusal/REVIEWS/branch_verdicts/uncovered_refusal;row_rulings/ROW_RULINGS/blessed_rows/BLESS_TAG;call_succeeded;requested_for/bind_pending/bound_kinds/bind_requested;record_outcome\"","-detail = \"A combined class accepts only amendment:<id>, first-approval:<id> and done-when:<id> Adjudicates tokens; it composes each named class through compose with that section's verdict path in fixed amendment, first-approval, done-when order, and refuses whole if any section cannot compose. One parser reads machine lines one physical line at a time: every line whose first token is a machine keyword counts, complete or not, so a bare, split, bad-label or field-missing line invalidates. The same module's review_line is the one reader of a review VERDICT line: exactly one case-insensitive VERDICT keyword line is accepted only as `VERDICT: <APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED> findings=<digits>`; the filing boundary refuses every other form and the merge reader treats it as no verdict. A combined sitting names exactly the kinds bound at reservation in <verdict>.requested, has exactly one kind-form lower-case section per bound kind and none other, and has across the whole file exactly one complete line per bound kind and one SITTING line, and no line for another kind; a single-kind verdict has no kind-form section and exactly one complete line for its kind. One binding records pending before either route calls: the coordinator entry point binds the request exclusively at reservation, and the loop binds its composed request at composition; write_binding is its one writer and record_outcome its one route recorder and commit path, returning its `(outcome, why)` decision and recording accepted only when call_succeeded has derived exit 0, no timeout and no reported error result, and validation passes. call_succeeded is derived once and shared by both routes; a failed call is recorded failed without its verdict being read, and the loop consumes that same why as its completion obligation. The coordinator validates its pending binding before recording its outcome, while the loop records against the kinds read back from its bound request; later consumers use the accepted-verdict reader, which returns only an accepted, bound, valid verdict and each part's row rulings. Every row an adjudication merge act flips needs an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part, and every row it re-attests needs a MEANING or CLARITY ruling in an accepted amendment part among the branch's own bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; an accepted verdict over other rows authorizes nothing, and a named verdict must judge each row its act re-attests. Failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdicts cover nothing. Ordinary and mixed lanes consume the same claimed-row reader at the merge: raw scope parsing, the complete acted set including ledger-only approvals, and the route’s normalized brief cannot give one consumer authority another does not see. Token and section parsing are shared by composition, validation, act scopes and merge-time minting; the class is routed and retainable through BRIEF_PROMPTS. An amendment verdict's `- [MEANING+BLESS]` row tag reads MEANING to every rulings reader, and a dedicated reader returns only rows so marked, treating a row without the mark or also tagged plain MEANING or CLARITY as not blessed.\"","+detail = \"A combined class accepts only amendment:<id>, first-approval:<id> and done-when:<id> Adjudicates tokens; it composes each named class through compose with that section's verdict path in fixed amendment, first-approval, done-when order, and refuses whole if any section cannot compose. One parser reads machine lines one physical line at a time: every line whose first token is a machine keyword counts, complete or not, so a bare, split, bad-label or field-missing line invalidates. The same module's review_line is the one reader of a review VERDICT line: exactly one case-insensitive VERDICT keyword line is accepted only as `VERDICT: <APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED> findings=<digits>`; the filing boundary refuses every other form and the merge reader treats it as no verdict. A combined sitting names exactly the kinds bound at reservation in <verdict>.requested, has exactly one kind-form lower-case section per bound kind and none other, and has across the whole file exactly one complete line per bound kind and one SITTING line, and no line for another kind; a single-kind verdict has no kind-form section and exactly one complete line for its kind. One binding records pending before either route calls: the coordinator entry point binds the request exclusively at reservation, and the loop binds its composed request at composition; write_binding is its one writer and record_outcome its one route recorder and commit path, returning its `(outcome, why)` decision and recording accepted only when call_succeeded has derived exit 0, no timeout and no reported error result, and validation passes. call_succeeded is derived once and shared by both routes; a failed call is recorded failed without its verdict being read, and the loop consumes that same why as its completion obligation. The coordinator validates its pending binding before recording its outcome, while the loop records against the kinds read back from its bound request; later consumers use the accepted-verdict reader, which returns only an accepted, bound, valid verdict and each part's row rulings. Every row an adjudication merge act flips needs an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part, and every row it re-attests needs a CLARITY ruling, or a MEANING ruling carrying the bless mark, in an accepted amendment part among the branch's own bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; an accepted verdict over other rows authorizes nothing, and a named verdict must authorize each row its act re-attests in the same way. Failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdicts cover nothing. Ordinary and mixed lanes consume the same claimed-row reader at the merge: raw scope parsing, the complete acted set including ledger-only approvals, and the route’s normalized brief cannot give one consumer authority another does not see. Token and section parsing are shared by composition, validation, act scopes and merge-time minting; the class is routed and retainable through BRIEF_PROMPTS. An amendment verdict's `- [MEANING+BLESS]` row tag reads MEANING to every rulings reader, and a dedicated reader returns only rows so marked, treating a row without the mark or also tagged plain MEANING or CLARITY as not blessed. One reader of the rows an accepted verdict authorizes, a MEANING row only when so marked, serves both the merge and the act assembler.\""," rationale = \"A shared checkpoint reduces adjudication launches only if no constituent judgement loses its scope or grammar. One parser prevents composition, validation and later acts from interpreting the same sitting differently. An adjudication machine line that repeats a declared field reads its last copy; only a model writing the same field twice on one line produces that form, which is outside the bound of lines the kit’s reviewer and adjudicator routes write.\""," test_refs = \"TC-327; TC-331; TC-368\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3463,"oldLines":7,"newStart":3463,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"Act assembly from a committed accepted verdict\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/adjudicate_act.py\""," code_symbol = \"ACTABLE/NONE_OWED/NO_ACT/RETIRE/_verdict_rel/_unaccepted_reason/_accepted/_tier/_held/_arm/_authorized/_Rows/_claims_approval/_retirement/_state/_odd/owed_acts/_recorded/_grouped/_derive/left_owed/bound_brief/_amendment_text/_approval_text/act_values/compose_act\"","-detail = \"From an accepted verdict and its binding as committed at a revision, the act assembler derives every authorized act that the revision's committed registries and records do not carry in full. A first-approval APPROVE act is derived only on a released rung; an amendment CLARITY act is derived on either rung with the verdict named when a listed row is held; an amendment MEANING act is derived only from a bless-marked row on a released rung. RETURN, unmarked or held MEANING, held APPROVE and Done-when verdict parts derive no act. Each authorized row is owed or taken by its committed status and whole-row recorded copy; any unrecognized state or unmatched tier refuses the whole derivation naming the row. An uncommitted, unbound, non-accepted or invalid verdict, a class deriving no act, a verdict with no remaining authorized act, or an approval without an approval record refuses the whole derivation naming why. The derived acts list amendment before first approval, and their brief is filled in full or not at all. At a revision, the later-act derivation reports the acts a verdict still owes or its refusal, and reports no outstanding act only when every authorized act is taken. It identifies the retained class from an accepted actable verdict binding. A re-attest of a row absent from the committed registries is a retirement: it is owed while its recorded copy holds the row and taken once the copy does not; on a held rung it derives no act. A first approval of an absent row is inconsistent and refuses naming the row. Each row's act is read from the one held-rung act policy that also informs the judging brief; the bless mark is the added condition for a released-rung MEANING row.\"","+detail = \"From an accepted verdict and its binding as committed at a revision, the act assembler derives every authorized act that the revision's committed registries and records do not carry in full. A first-approval APPROVE act is derived only on a released rung; an amendment CLARITY act is derived on either rung with the verdict named when a listed row is held; an amendment MEANING act is derived only from a bless-marked row on a released rung. RETURN, unmarked or held MEANING, held APPROVE and Done-when verdict parts derive no act. Each authorized row is owed or taken by its committed status and whole-row recorded copy; any unrecognized state or unmatched tier refuses the whole derivation naming the row. An uncommitted, unbound, non-accepted or invalid verdict, a class deriving no act, a verdict with no remaining authorized act, or an approval without an approval record refuses the whole derivation naming why. The derived acts list amendment before first approval, and their brief is filled in full or not at all. At a revision, the later-act derivation reports the acts a verdict still owes or its refusal, and reports no outstanding act only when every authorized act is taken. It identifies the retained class from an accepted actable verdict binding. A re-attest of a row absent from the committed registries is a retirement: it is owed while its recorded copy holds the row and taken once the copy does not; on a held rung it derives no act. A first approval of an absent row is inconsistent and refuses naming the row. Which rows a verdict authorizes, a MEANING row only with its bless mark, is read from the one reader the merge's backing check also reads, and each authorized row's act from the one held-rung act policy that also informs the judging brief.\""," rationale = \"A later act must preserve the accepted verdict's authority while refusing a partial or stale approval operation.\""," test_refs = \"TC-368\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-943\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\test\\test-cases.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":2827,"oldLines":11,"newStart":2827,"newLines":11,"lines":[" [test.TC-278]"," verifies = [\"SR-178\", \"SR-228\", \"LLR-278\", \"IF-091\"]"," level = \"Integration\"","-method = \"Driven on real git repositories made from scaffolds. An adjudication lane claiming an amendment row scoped to one requirement, whose act re-attests two amended requirements, is refused at merge naming the one outside the scope; an act re-attesting only the scoped row merges, and the same act claimed by a first-approval row is refused naming the row, since that lane holds no re-attestation scope. The held authority is read at trunk's tip rather than the merge base. A combined act across the same or different registries merges when each section acts only within its own kind scope and every copied registry has an acted row, including a ledger-only approval; a copied registry neither section moved is WIDENED. With the rung held, a re-attestation without a verdict, of a row below approval, with a MEANING verdict, with a verdict that does not rule the row, with a named verdict file absent at the act's head, with an accepted verdict that is invalid as a whole, or with a verdict tagging the row both MEANING and CLARITY is refused by row, while an accepted verdict ruling an approved row CLARITY merges. On a RELEASED rung, an act naming an unaccepted verdict is refused by the verdict named, and its whole act, a first approval included, is refused. Each act permitted to merge in these cases carries branch bindings that judge every row it flips APPROVE and every row it re-attests MEANING or CLARITY, written by _bind_branch; a named verdict must itself be accepted and judge each row its act re-attests, and omitting --verdict does not waive the branch binding. A first-approval part that RETURNs its only judged row approves nothing, while its combined act may re-attest its amendment row. One act that approves a Drafted requirement and re-attests an amended one, claimed by a first-approval row scoped to the first and an amendment row scoped to the second, merges; the same act whose amendment row is scoped to another requirement is refused naming the re-attested row. On a RELEASED rung, an act whose only re-attestation retires an Approved row the lane deleted, ruled by an accepted amendment verdict inside the amendment scope, merges with that registry's copy as its only copy. The same retirement on a held rung is refused by row.\"","+method = \"Driven on real git repositories made from scaffolds. An adjudication lane claiming an amendment row scoped to one requirement, whose act re-attests two amended requirements, is refused at merge naming the one outside the scope; an act re-attesting only the scoped row merges, and the same act claimed by a first-approval row is refused naming the row, since that lane holds no re-attestation scope. The held authority is read at trunk's tip rather than the merge base. A combined act across the same or different registries merges when each section acts only within its own kind scope and every copied registry has an acted row, including a ledger-only approval; a copied registry neither section moved is WIDENED. With the rung held, a re-attestation without a verdict, of a row below approval, with a MEANING verdict, with a verdict that does not rule the row, with a named verdict file absent at the act's head, with an accepted verdict that is invalid as a whole, or with a verdict tagging the row both MEANING and CLARITY is refused by row, while an accepted verdict ruling an approved row CLARITY merges. On a RELEASED rung, an act naming an unaccepted verdict is refused by the verdict named, and its whole act, a first approval included, is refused. Each act permitted to merge in these cases carries branch bindings that judge every row it flips APPROVE and every row it re-attests CLARITY, or MEANING with the bless mark, written by _bind_branch; a named verdict must itself be accepted and authorize each row its act re-attests in the same way, and omitting --verdict does not waive the branch binding. On a RELEASED rung, an act re-attesting a row that its accepted amendment verdict rules plain MEANING, or tags MEANING+BLESS and another way, is refused naming the row, through the backing check and through a named verdict alike. A combined act carrying one is refused whole. The same act under a MEANING+BLESS ruling merges. A first-approval part that RETURNs its only judged row approves nothing, while its combined act may re-attest its amendment row. One act that approves a Drafted requirement and re-attests an amended one, claimed by a first-approval row scoped to the first and an amendment row scoped to the second, merges; the same act whose amendment row is scoped to another requirement is refused naming the re-attested row. On a RELEASED rung, an act whose only re-attestation retires an Approved row the lane deleted, ruled by an accepted amendment verdict inside the amendment scope, merges with that registry's copy as its only copy. The same retirement on a held rung is refused by row.\""," tier = \"Full\"","-expected = \"Satisfies LLR-278 (parents SR-178 and SR-228): a re-attestation outside the amendment row's Adjudicates scope is refused at merge by name; on a held rung only an accepted verdict ruling the row CLARITY permits it; every acted approval, including a ledger-only approval, needs its approved rows APPROVE-ruled and its re-attested rows MEANING- or CLARITY-ruled by accepted branch bindings, and a named verdict judges each row it re-attests; and an act whose approvals and re-attestations each lie in the scope of the row claiming them merges. A retired row's re-attestation authorizes its own registry's copy, whether or not another act moved that registry.\"","+expected = \"Satisfies LLR-278 (parents SR-178 and SR-228): a re-attestation outside the amendment row's Adjudicates scope is refused at merge by name; on a held rung only an accepted verdict ruling the row CLARITY permits it; every acted approval, including a ledger-only approval, needs its approved rows APPROVE-ruled and its re-attested rows CLARITY-ruled, or MEANING-ruled with the bless mark, by accepted branch bindings, and a named verdict authorizes each row it re-attests in the same way; and an act whose approvals and re-attestations each lie in the scope of the row claiming them merges. A retired row's re-attestation authorizes its own registry's copy, whether or not another act moved that registry.\""," automated = \"Yes\"","-evidence = \"tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_reattestation_outside_the_amendment_scope_is_refused_by_name; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_reattestation_inside_the_amendment_scope_merges; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_without_a_verdict_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_its_verdict_rules_CLARITY_merges; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_of_a_MEANING_row_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_is_read_from_trunk_not_from_the_merge_base; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_of_a_DRAFTED_row_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_its_verdict_does_not_rule_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_reattestation_whose_verdict_is_unreadable_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_held_rung_row_ruled_both_ways_reads_MEANING_and_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_mixed_approval_and_reattestation_act_merges; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_mixed_act_reattesting_outside_its_amendment_scope_is_refused; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_combined_sittings_sections_each_take_their_own_act; tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_a_combined_sittings_act_outside_its